md breaks one file across two lines when the value holds a newline.

The README describes --key as "path<TAB>value lines, one per file", and that
shape is the whole reason --key exists: it is the output a script cuts on the
tab. The fixture here holds one file whose `note` is a YAML literal block:

    note: |
      first
      second

so the value is `first\nsecond`. md prints it raw, and the output becomes

    <path><TAB>first
    second

Two lines for one file. A reader that takes the field after the first tab of
each line gets `first` for this file and then a line with no tab at all, which
it will either drop or mistake for another file. There is no way to tell that
second line apart from a path whose value is missing.

`md lines --count` reports 2 for that. One record for the one file would be 1.

The cause is ListCommand.renderPlain, which for the --key branch writes
`"\(entry.path)\t\(formatScalarValue(value))\n"`, and formatScalarValue ends at
`"\(normalized)"` for a scalar: no escaping, no quoting, no check for a newline.

Any fix that keeps one record on one line closes this. Escaping the newline as
\\n is the smallest one, and matches what the tab-separated shape already
implies. If instead the fix quotes the whole value, the expected count stays 1,
so this case still holds.
